                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Eight Dragons is a retro-inspired side-scrolling beat 'em up developed and published by Extend Mode that confidently embraces the spirit of the arcade era while incorporating modern conveniences and ambitious multiplayer features. Rather than attempting to modernize the genre with elaborate progression systems or cinematic storytelling, the game focuses on delivering straightforward, action-packed combat that recalls the golden age of arcade brawlers. From the moment players step into its colorful pixel-art world, the experience is built around fast action, cooperative fun, and the satisfaction of clearing streets filled with enemies. Its premise is simple but effective: the city has fallen under the control of the ruthless crime boss Kane, and it is up to the legendary Eight Dragons to fight through waves of criminals and restore order.\n\nOne of the game's biggest strengths lies in its diverse cast of playable heroes. Each member of the Eight Dragons has their own personality and individual storyline, giving players a reason to revisit the campaign multiple times. Although the overarching objective remains the same, the narrative unfolds from different perspectives depending on the chosen character, gradually revealing how their journeys intersect. This interconnected storytelling adds a surprising amount of replay value without distracting from the game's primary focus on action. While the plot is intentionally uncomplicated, it serves as an enjoyable backdrop that keeps the adventure moving forward and gives each character a distinct place within the larger conflict.\n\nCombat remains faithful to classic beat 'em up traditions, emphasizing responsive controls, satisfying melee attacks, and continuous encounters against large groups of enemies. Players can unleash combinations of punches and kicks, throw opponents across the screen, and make use of weapons found throughout each stage to gain an advantage. The action steadily intensifies as stronger enemies and memorable bosses begin to appear, requiring players to balance aggressive offense with careful positioning. Although the mechanics are intentionally accessible, mastering crowd control and making smart use of available resources creates enough depth to keep battles engaging throughout the campaign. The pacing rarely slows, making every level feel energetic and rewarding for players who enjoy nonstop arcade-style action.\n\nThe standout feature that separates Eight Dragons from many other modern brawlers is its impressive multiplayer support. The game allows up to eight players to join together either locally or online, creating an exciting level of chaos that few titles in the genre attempt. Large cooperative sessions become packed with constant action as players coordinate attacks, revive momentum during overwhelming encounters, and experience multiple character storylines simultaneously. Even when played alone, the game remains enjoyable, but the cooperative component elevates the experience considerably. The ability to accommodate such a large group of players gives Eight Dragons a unique identity and makes it an appealing choice for parties or friends looking for a classic arcade experience with modern online functionality.\n\nVisually, the game captures the charm of old-school arcade titles through vibrant pixel art, expressive character animations, and varied stage designs that successfully recreate the atmosphere of classic side-scrolling brawlers. The soundtrack complements the action with energetic music that maintains excitement from beginning to end. Adjustable difficulty levels and customizable game speed also make the experience more flexible, allowing both newcomers and seasoned beat 'em up veterans to tailor the challenge to their preferences. These quality-of-life features improve accessibility while preserving the game's old-school identity, ensuring that players can enjoy the experience at their own pace.\n\nDespite its many strengths, Eight Dragons is not without limitations. Its dedication to traditional arcade design means the gameplay loop can become repetitive over longer sessions, especially for solo players. Those seeking deep character customization, complex combo systems, or an emotionally driven narrative may find the experience somewhat limited. Enemy encounters occasionally rely on familiar patterns, and the campaign structure remains relatively straightforward regardless of the selected character. However, these shortcomings stem more from the game's commitment to its classic inspirations than from poor design, and fans of retro beat 'em ups are likely to appreciate its focused approach rather than view it as a drawback.\n\nOverall, Eight Dragons succeeds by understanding exactly what makes classic arcade brawlers enjoyable and delivering that formula with confidence. Its responsive combat, entertaining cast of characters, interconnected storylines, attractive retro presentation, and exceptional eight-player cooperative mode create a package that feels both nostalgic and refreshing. While it may not redefine the genre, it offers an authentic, highly enjoyable experience that celebrates the best elements of classic side-scrolling action games while adding enough modern features to remain engaging for today's players.\n\nRating: 7/10",
